The && operator returns the first falsy operand or the last value if all are truthy. The || operator returns the first truthy operand or the last value if all are falsy. Neither evaluates the remaining operands once it has decided.

code:
const name = user?.name || 'guest';
const ok = isAuth && isAdmin;

real example:
A config fallback like const baseURL = process.env.URL || 'http://localhost:3000' gives a repeatable test default whenever the variable is unset.

Use String(value), Number(value), and Boolean(value). These constructor-style conversions make intent clear compared to relying on implicit coercion.

code:
const s = String(42);
const n = Number('42');
const b = Boolean(1);

real example:
Serializing a test ID with String(id) before placing it in a dynamic selector guarantees a clean match against DOM attributes.
